public class Data_Types {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        System.out.println("Learning Topic: Data_Types");
        System.out.println("In Java, there are several primitive data types that are used to store different kinds of data. These include:");
        System.out.println("- byte: A 8-bit signed integer that can store values from -128 to 127.");
        System.out.println("- short: A 16-bit signed integer that can store values from -32,768 to 32,767.");
        System.out.println("- int: A 32-bit signed integer that can store values from -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647.");
        System.out.println("- long: A 64-bit signed integer that can store values from -9,223,372,036,854,775,808 to 9,223,372,036,854,775,807.");
        System.out.println("- float: A 32-bit floating-point number that can store decimal values with single precision.");
        System.out.println("- double: A 64-bit floating-point number that can store decimal values with double precision.");
        System.out.println("- char: A 16-bit Unicode character that can store a single character or a Unicode symbol.");
        System.out.println("- boolean: A data type that can store only two values: true or false.");
        System.out.println("In addition to primitive data types, Java also has reference data types, which are used to store objects and arrays. Reference data types include:");
        System.out.println("- String: A class that represents a sequence of characters. Strings are immutable, meaning that once they are created, they cannot be changed.");
        System.out.println("- Arrays: A data structure that can store a fixed number of values of the same type. Arrays are indexed, meaning that each element can be accessed using its index.");
        System.out.println("- Classes and Objects: In Java, you can create your own classes to define custom data types. Objects are instances of classes that can have their own properties and behaviors.");
        System.out.println("Understanding data types is essential for writing efficient and effective Java programs. It allows you to choose the appropriate data type for your variables and ensures that your program can handle the data correctly. Now you can try using different data types in your Java programs!");         

    }
}
